<div class="callout-icon">⚡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Kenyon Sadiq ran a <strong>4.39-second 40-yard dash</strong> at the 2026 NFL Combine — the fastest ever recorded by a tight end. His Oregon teammate Eli Stowers posted a <strong>45.5-inch vertical jump</strong>, the best mark by any tight end dating back to at least 2003. Together, the pair turned the tight end position group into one of the biggest storylines of combine week.</div>
</div>
<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<h2>A Tight End Class Unlike Any Other</h2>
<p>Tight end is typically one of the less-hyped position groups at the combine — fewer elite prospects, fewer viral testing numbers. That changed in 2026, when the drills turned into what evaluators called a heavyweight battle between two Oregon products: Eli Stowers and Kenyon Sadiq. Both delivered elite athletic testing across the board, and together they reset what's considered possible for the position.</p>
<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<h2>Sadiq's Record-Setting 40</h2>
<p>Kenyon Sadiq ran a <strong>4.39-second 40-yard dash</strong> — the fastest time ever recorded by a tight end at the NFL Combine. For context, the average tight end at this year's combine ran 4.63 seconds, meaning Sadiq beat the position average by nearly a quarter of a second, a massive gap at that distance.</p>
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Benchmark</th>
