</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="elite"><td>Kenyon Sadiq (2026, combine TE record)</td><td>4.39s</td></tr>
<tr><td>2026 combine TE average</td><td>4.63s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Good TE 40 time (NFL level)</td><td>Under 4.6s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elite TE 40 time (NFL level)</td><td>Under 4.5s</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Speed at tight end at this level changes how a defense has to account for the position — a tight end who runs 4.39 forces safeties and linebackers into coverage decisions usually reserved for wide receivers. Speed alone doesn't guarantee production, but it changes what an offense can ask a player to do.</p>
<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<h2>Stowers' Historic Vertical Jump</h2>
<p>While Sadiq owned the sprint, his teammate Eli Stowers owned the jump. Stowers posted a <strong>45.5-inch vertical jump</strong> — the best mark recorded by any tight end dating back to at least 2003, when modern combine record-keeping became standardized. That kind of explosiveness shows up most directly in contested catches and red-zone situations, where the ability to out-jump a defender at the catch point matters more than straight-line speed.</p>
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Benchmark</th>
