<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2>What This Means Going Into the Season</h2>
<p>Testing numbers this extreme tend to shift how a team plans to use a player. A tight end who runs 4.39 or jumps 45.5 inches isn't just a blocker who can catch a checkdown — both profiles suggest players built to be featured as receiving threats from day one. Whichever teams land Sadiq and Stowers will likely be under pressure to get them on the field early, given how rare their testing profiles are at the position.</p>
<p>As with any combine standout, the real evaluation starts once pads come on. Straight-line speed and a max-effort vertical are measured in isolation; NFL production depends on route-running, hands, and blocking — the parts of the position the combine doesn't test directly.</p>
